#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Benchmark CodeSage semantic search against ground-truth YAML corpora.

Every run executes a uniform-file seeded placebo at the exact CodeSage
path/content byte budget, a real path-sorted literal-token rg floor, and
that rg page cut at the CodeSage budget. JSON records carry all four arms,
scores, byte costs, and evidence. Missing/failed arms invalidate the run.
The runner pins actual source bytes, eligible semantic files/chunks, the
persisted semantic fingerprint, model/runtime artifact bytes, config, and
binary bytes before and after retrieval. A stale semantic index is refused.
Executable arguments resolve once against the caller's cwd/PATH; every
version, status, search, and rg child uses the resulting absolute identity.
For a configured reranker, repeat --reranker-artifact with the tokenizer
and the selected ONNX graph; verify selection against the binary's loader
pins. Each query uses a fresh private runtime directory, observes private
model initialization, and rejects daemon reuse. This instrument does not
measure indexing pass mode, prospective prediction, or agent completion.

Emits a scorecard in the "named repo + named model + named baseline + named
quantities" shape adopted from the external landscape sweep (see
notes/20260424-reference-tool-recommendations.md, item 1.6). Every public
claim derived from this runner should be quotable verbatim from the output.

Usage:
  codesage-bench-runner <corpus.yaml> [--limit 10] [--verbose] [--codesage-bin PATH]
      [--results-json out.json] [--split train|heldout --salt STR]
      [--allow-search-failures]

`--results-json` writes `{"meta": {...}, "complete": bool, "records": [...]}`
for `bench/compare-runs.py`. Each record is `{id, query, expected_files,
hits (ranked file list), first_hit_rank | null, source, repo}` plus
`error: "timeout" | "rc=N" | "error"` when that case's `codesage search` did
not run cleanly. `meta` carries `corpus` (basename), `corpus_sha256`, `split`,
`salt`, `limit`, `head` (project HEAD), `model`, `reranker`, `codesage`
(first banner line), `build_target`, `features`, `device`, `run_at`,
`project_root`, and `search_failures` (count of records with `error`). The
path is probed before any search runs and the file is rewritten after every
case with `complete: false` until the run finishes, so an interrupted run
keeps its partial records.

Search failures are double-penalised on purpose: a failed search is scored as
a miss in every aggregate (miss rate, recall, MRR, tokens) and excluded from
none of them, the count is appended to METRICS as `search_failures=N`, the
one-liner is prefixed `[INVALID: N search failures]`, and the process exits 3
unless `--allow-search-failures`. A broken index must never read as "0% hit
rate" in a saved scorecard.

`--split` keeps only the cases whose salted sha256 lands in that half (same
`split_of` as compare-runs.py); tune on `train`, run `heldout` once, and
record the salt next to the result.

Exit codes: 0 clean run; 1 unwritable `--results-json` or missing
`project_root`; 2 usage error, malformed corpus, or no cases to run (empty
`cases:` or a `--split` that selects none); 3 search failures without
`--allow-search-failures`.

The METRICS comment names the binary as two whitespace-free tokens,
`codesage=<version> build=<release|debug>` (`build=` omitted when the banner
has no suffix), so single-line `key=value` consumers keep working.
"""

